PLACES=[
N('fort-sisseton','Fort Sisseton (Fort Wadsworth)','Lake City','sd','fort','standing',45.65778,-97.53056,'site','open','walk','forts','1864–1889',
  'An Army post founded in 1864 on the Coteau des Prairies, first called Fort Wadsworth and renamed Fort Sisseton in 1876, after the Sisseton band of the Dakota, because New York already had a Fort Wadsworth. Cavalry and infantry held it until 1889; companies of the 25th Infantry, an African-American regiment, were there from 1884 to 1888. The log buildings were replaced in brick and stone, and the state made the fort a historic state park in 1959.',
  'Fourteen original buildings stand round the parade ground (Wikipedia, from the Library of Congress): officers\' quarters, the stone barracks, the powder magazine, the guard house, the adjutant\'s office, the commanding officer\'s two-storey brick house of 1866 and a cross-shaped library and school (state park page; Register form). The Register form says much of the brick was made at the fort\'s own kiln. A visitor centre has displays; buildings are shown by guided tour in summer. Interior fabric not checked.',
  'A prairie fort built of its own brick.',
  accn='Fort Sisseton Historic State Park, SD Game, Fish and Parks, 11907 434th Avenue. From Eden go west 5 miles on Broadway Avenue/122nd Street, north 3 miles on 434th Avenue, and use the north entrance (park page). The park is open all year; daily licence $10 for residents, $15 for others. A state park festival is held each June. Hours from the park page, read November 2026; for other tours call the park office.',
  links=[W('Fort Sisseton'),NOM('73001745'),['SD Game, Fish and Parks: Fort Sisseton Historic State Park','https://gfp.sd.gov/parks/detail/fort-sisseton-historic-state-park/']],
  th=[45.65902,-97.52976],thn='Park lot off 434th Avenue',brick='?',
  hours='Park all year. Visitor centre daily from Open House weekend (mid-May) to Labor Day, tours 10:30, 1:30 and 4; after that Monday to Friday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('fort-randall-chapel','Fort Randall chapel ruins','Pickstown','sd','worship','ruins',43.04946,-98.56284,'site','public','walk','forts','1875–',
  'Fort Randall was founded by General William S. Harney in June 1856 on the west bank of the Missouri and held longer than any other post on the upper river; Sitting Bull and his band were kept near it from 1881 to 1883. In 1875 soldiers of the 1st Infantry built a cross-shaped chapel that also held a library and an Odd Fellows lodge room, designed by the post carpenter George Bush and paid for by off-duty soldiers and local people. When the Army left in 1892 every other building was sold at auction and carried off; only the chapel was left.',
  'The roofless walls of the chapel, of hand-sawn chalkstone from nearby quarries (HMdb), held up since 2003 by a steel frame the Corps of Engineers put over it after an NPS study. A half-mile paved walk round the parade ground passes the outlines of the other buildings, with interpretive signs (NPS). The post cemetery and an overlook are on the bluff to the west (HMdb).',
  'The one building nobody bought at the auction.',
  accn='US Army Corps of Engineers land below Fort Randall Dam, in the Missouri National Recreational River; the markers are on Stone Church Drive east of Toe Road West (HMdb). Open ground; no hours were found on a page of the Corps. Pin on the sheltered building beside the loop road; see the pin note.',
  links=[W('Fort Randall'),['NPS: Fort Randall','https://www.nps.gov/mnrr/learn/historyculture/fortrandall.htm'],HM('188297','Spiritual and Social Sustenance'),HM('188367','Fort Randall chapel'),HM('235026','Fort Randall overlook')],
  brick='no'),
N('corn-palace-mitchell','Corn Palace','Mitchell','sd','civic','standing',43.71478,-98.0254,'exact','open','drive','','1921–',
  'Mitchell built its first Corn Palace in 1892 to show off the soil and draw settlers, rebuilt it in 1905, and built the present hall in 1921 to a design by Rapp and Rapp of Chicago. The onion domes and minarets were added in 1937. Of at least thirty-four corn palaces built across the Midwest between 1887 and 1930, it is the only one left. It is the town\'s arena and hall.',
  'Murals of corn and other grains cover the outside and are stripped and made new every year to a fresh theme; Oscar Howe designed them from 1948 to 1971 (Wikipedia). The domes were redone in metal in 2015. Inside: displays on the murals and the building\'s past; free guided tours in summer (Corn Palace page). Interior fabric not checked.',
  'The last corn palace, re-dressed every year.',
  accn='City of Mitchell, 604 North Main Street. Free admission and parking; no overnight parking. Closed on New Year\'s Day, Martin Luther King Day, Presidents\' Day, Easter, Native American Day, Veterans Day, Thanksgiving and Christmas, and hours change during the Corn Palace Festival (Corn Palace page, read November 2026).',
  links=[W('Corn Palace'),['Corn Palace: About us and hours','https://www.cornpalace.com/27/About-Us']],
  brick='?',hours='Memorial Day to Labor Day daily 8 a.m. to 9 p.m.; May, September and October daily 8 to 5; November to April Monday to Saturday 8 to 5'),
N('meridian-bridge-yankton','Meridian Bridge','Yankton','sd','landmark','standing',42.86458,-97.39397,'site','public','walk','','1924–',
  'A double-deck bridge across the Missouri between Yankton and Cedar County, Nebraska, finished in the summer of 1924 and dedicated as a toll bridge in October that year. It replaced a ferry of 1870 and a pontoon bridge that was taken apart and rebuilt twice a year. It was built for trains below and cars above, with a lift span for boats; tolls ended in 1953 and the two decks became one-way roads. The lift gear and counterweights came out in the 1980s, it closed to traffic in 2008, and it reopened for walkers and cyclists in 2011.',
  'The two-level truss over the river, now a walking and cycling path with a two-block plaza at the Yankton end (Wikipedia). The river below is part of the Missouri National Recreational River.',
  'Cars on top, trains below, now just people.',
  accn='A public path from downtown Yankton; parking at Riverside Park on Park Drive and on the streets near the bridge. Hours not checked.',
  links=[W('Meridian Highway Bridge')],
  th=[42.86640,-97.39144],thn='Riverside Park lot, Park Drive',brick='?'),
N('prairie-homestead-sod-house','Prairie Homestead sod house','Philip','sd','landmark','standing',43.80111,-101.90639,'site','open','drive','','1909–',
  'A sod house built in 1909 by Ed Brown and his wife, of blocks of prairie sod under a grass roof. Western South Dakota was among the last ground in the state to be homesteaded, and this is one of the few sod houses left in it. Listed on the National Register in 1974.',
  'The sod house itself, open for tours, with farm animals and a prairie-dog town on the grounds (Wikipedia). Interior fabric not checked.',
  'A house cut out of the prairie.',
  accn='A private attraction at 21070 SD Highway 240: from I-90 exit 131 go 2½ miles south on Highway 240, half a mile north of Badlands National Park (attraction\'s page). Admission and hours not checked: the attraction\'s pages are drawn by script and could not be read.',
  links=[W('Prairie Homestead'),['Prairie Homestead: getting here','https://www.prairiehomestead.com/getting-here/']],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('minuteman-delta-09-silo','Minuteman II silo Delta-09','Wall','sd','fort','standing',43.93167,-102.16006,'exact','open','drive','','1963–',
  'A launch facility of the 66th Strategic Missile Squadron, built in 1963 and run from Ellsworth Air Force Base near Rapid City. After the arms-reduction treaty the wing was stood down in 1994 and every site in the complex was destroyed except this silo and the Delta-01 control centre. It is now part of Minuteman Missile National Historic Site.',
  'The silo is 12 feet across and 80 feet deep, of reinforced concrete lined with steel; the door is fixed half open under a glass viewing enclosure, so you look down on a disarmed Minuteman II missile (Wikipedia). The fenced top-side compound is toured on foot with a phone audio tour; there is no way down.',
  'A missile you can look down on, and so can satellites.',
  accn='Minuteman Missile National Historic Site (NPS), about half a mile southwest of I-90 exit 116, 6 miles from Wall. NPS says visitors may tour the site daily; the times are on its hours page, which is drawn by script and was not read. Free. The visitor centre is at exit 131.',
  links=[W('Minuteman Missile National Historic Site'),MIMI_9],
  th=[43.93285,-102.15947],thn='Delta-09 lot on 195th Avenue',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('minuteman-delta-01-control','Minuteman launch control facility Delta-01','Philip','sd','fort','standing',43.87778,-101.96154,'site','open','drive','','',
  'A launch control facility of the 66th Strategic Missile Squadron, about 10½ miles east-southeast of the Delta-09 silo. It was one of the two sites in the complex kept when the rest were destroyed after 1994.',
  'The topside building and, by guided tour only, the underground launch control capsule, which takes six people at a time for about half an hour (Wikipedia). The gate is kept locked as it was when the site was active.',
  'A control capsule six people fit into.',
  accn='Minuteman Missile National Historic Site (NPS), about 1.7 miles north of I-90 exit 127. No self-guided visits: tours are offered daily and are booked on recreation.gov (NPS guided tours and fees pages, read November 2026). Tour times not checked.',
  links=[W('Minuteman Missile National Historic Site'),MIMI_T,MIMI_F],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('historic-adams-house-deadwood','Historic Adams House','Deadwood','sd','landmark','standing',44.37212,-103.72672,'exact','open','walk','','1892–',
  'A Queen Anne house built in 1892 for Harris and Anna Franklin, a sign that the rough gold camp of 1876 had money and society. W. E. Adams, who founded the Adams Museum in 1930, bought it in 1920. Deadwood as a whole is a National Historic Landmark district for its gold-rush buildings.',
  'Shown furnished with the Adams family\'s own furniture and restored interiors (Wikipedia); the city says more than 80 per cent of the furnishings belonged to the Adams family. Christmas tours in December.',
  'A gold-camp fortune, furnished as they left it.',
  accn='Deadwood History Inc., 22 Van Buren Avenue. Admission charged; a $22 pass covers the Adams Museum, the Days of \'76 Museum and the house (Deadwood History page, read November 2026). Closed November to March except for Christmas tours.',
  links=[W('Adams Museum & House'),DHI,['City of Deadwood: Historic Adams House','https://cityofdeadwood.com/node/26921'],W('Deadwood, South Dakota')],
  brick='?',hours='May to September daily 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.; October and April Tuesday to Saturday 10 to 4; last tour 4 p.m.; closed November to March'),
N('mount-moriah-cemetery-deadwood','Mount Moriah Cemetery','Deadwood','sd','burial','standing',44.37599,-103.72599,'area','open','walk','cemeteries','1878–',
  'Laid out in 1878 by the Lawrence County Commission on the mountainside above Deadwood. Wild Bill Hickok, Calamity Jane and Seth Bullock are buried here. Many of the graves were moved up from the earlier Ingleside Cemetery in the 1880s when that ground was wanted for houses, and not all were found.',
  'Terraced sections on the steep hill, among them potter\'s fields, a mass grave for eleven men killed in a sawmill fire, and a children\'s section (Wikipedia). By tradition the flag flies day and night. A visitor centre shows a short film and has panels on the cemetery\'s history (city page). Allow at least half an hour for the well-known graves.',
  'Where the Wild West was buried.',
  accn='City of Deadwood. $2 entrance, cash only. Large vehicles park in the Sherman Street lot. In winter only limited maintenance (city page, read November 2026). The visitor centre is open Memorial Day to mid-October, 8 a.m. to 8 p.m.',
  links=[W('Mount Moriah Cemetery (South Dakota)'),['City of Deadwood: Mount Moriah Cemetery','https://www.cityofdeadwood.com/node/3461']],
  conf='',brick='?',hours='Mid-May to mid-October daily 8 a.m. to 8 p.m.; limited in winter; $2 cash'),
N('mount-roosevelt-friendship-tower','Mount Roosevelt Friendship Tower','Deadwood','sd','landmark','standing',44.39806,-103.75917,'site','public','hike','','1919–',
  'Seth Bullock, Deadwood\'s first sheriff, met Theodore Roosevelt while chasing horse thieves, and the two stayed friends for life. When Roosevelt died in 1919 Bullock had this tower built with the Society of the Black Hills Pioneers, and it was dedicated on July 4, 1919, the first memorial to Roosevelt anywhere. The Society gave it to the Forest Service in 1966.',
  'A round observation tower of local stone, 31 feet high with its six-foot platform, on the summit of Mount Roosevelt. A 2010 restoration steadied the foundation and repaired the stone, and added steps and handrails up to the platform (Wikipedia; SHPO hikes itinerary).',
  'Two friends, a horse-thief chase and a stone tower.',
  accn='Black Hills National Forest. The trail starts at the Mount Roosevelt Picnic Area on Mount Roosevelt Road, reached by Forest Service Road 133 off US 85 (State Historic Preservation Office itinerary). Hours not checked.',
  links=[W('Mount Theodore Roosevelt Monument'),['SD State Historic Preservation Office: Black Hills hikes itinerary','https://history.sd.gov/preservation/docs/BlackHillsHikes_Itinerary.pdf']],
  th=[44.39692,-103.75445],thn='Mount Roosevelt Picnic Area lot',brick='no'),
N('dc-booth-fish-hatchery','D.C. Booth Historic National Fish Hatchery','Spearfish','sd','industry','standing',44.48121,-103.86015,'site','open','walk','','1896–',
  'Founded in 1896 to put trout into the streams of the Black Hills; a hard winter held up building, and the hatchery was finished by July 1899, with the first trout let into Little Spearfish and Whitewood creeks in 1900. D. C. Booth ran it for 34 years. It still raises trout and keeps the national archive of fish culture.',
  'The 1899 hatchery building, the oldest on the grounds, now the fisheries museum; the Booth House of 1905, a wood-frame Colonial Revival house on a rusticated sandstone base with columned porches and oval windows, shown with period furniture; a replica fish railway car; ponds and trails to the old reservoirs (Fish and Wildlife Service; Wikipedia). The ice house is a replica.',
  'Trout for the Black Hills, by rail car.',
  accn='US Fish and Wildlife Service, on Spearfish Creek in Spearfish. Free; the attractions are run with volunteers and hours may change without notice (FWS pages, read November 2026). The canyon road to Canyon Street by Winterville Road is closed to through traffic in summer.',
  links=[W('D.C. Booth Historic National Fish Hatchery'),['FWS: D.C. Booth Historic National Fish Hatchery','https://www.fws.gov/fish-hatchery/dc-booth-historic'],['FWS: tours','https://www.fws.gov/locations/dc-booth-national-fish-hatchery/tours']],
  brick='?',hours='Grounds dawn to dusk all year; fish car tours mid-May to September daily 9 a.m. to 6 p.m.; museum and Booth House in business hours'),
N('battle-mountain-sanitarium','Battle Mountain Sanitarium','Hot Springs','sd','inst','standing',43.43639,-103.47889,'site','view','drive','','1907–',
  'A branch of the National Home for Disabled Volunteer Soldiers, set up by law in 1902 and opened in 1907, the only one that was purely a hospital. It treated old soldiers with joint and bone ailments in the mineral springs and tuberculosis in the dry air. It passed to the Veterans Administration in 1930 and is now the Hot Springs campus of the VA Black Hills Health Care System; most of it became a National Historic Landmark in 2011.',
  'Buildings of local pink sandstone by Thomas Rogers Kimball, a mix of Mission Revival and Romanesque, on a bluff over the Fall River; the main building is a round centre with wings like spokes. The grounds were laid out by George Kessler (Wikipedia). The VA has proposed closing the campus, and its future has been in question (NPS article); present use not checked. Interior not checked.',
  'A hospital that bet on the springs.',
  accn='VA Black Hills Health Care System, a working medical campus on the northeast side of Hot Springs. Look from the public streets; going in for a look was not checked.',
  links=[W('Battle Mountain Sanitarium'),['NPS: Battle Mountain Sanitarium faces uncertain future','https://www.nps.gov/articles/battle-mountain-sanitarium-faces-uncertain-future.htm']],
  brick='no'),
N('mellette-house-watertown','Mellette House','Watertown','sd','landmark','standing',44.90727,-97.12155,'exact','open','walk','','1885–',
  'Built in 1885 on Prospect Hill for Arthur C. Mellette, the first governor of South Dakota, who lived here until 1895. Watertown\'s first radio station was in it in 1929. Condemned in 1943, it was bought by a group that became the Mellette Memorial Association and restored it.',
  'A two-storey house shown with Mellette family pictures and original furniture and household things (Wikipedia). Free tours in summer. Interior fabric beyond that not checked.',
  'The first governor\'s house, saved from the wreckers.',
  accn='Mellette Memorial Association, 421 Fifth Avenue Northwest. Free tours in summer (Wikipedia); no page of the association was found, so days and times were not checked.',
  links=[W('Mellette House')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('fort-meade-sturgis','Fort Meade','Sturgis','sd','fort','standing',44.41085,-103.47144,'area','open','drive','forts','1878–1944',
  'Founded in 1878 as Camp Sturgis, then Camp Ruhlen, to guard the Deadwood gold country; the post was built from August 1878 to August 1879. The 7th Cavalry was here after the Little Bighorn, as were Buffalo Soldiers of the 25th Infantry and, in the Second World War, the 88th Glider Infantry. An officer of the 8th Cavalry here began standing for the Star-Spangled Banner at ceremonies long before it was the anthem. The Army left in 1944; the post is now a VA hospital and National Guard ground, and much of the old reservation is BLM land.',
  'The fort\'s first timber buildings are mostly gone, replaced early in the 1900s by brick and stone ones (Wikipedia). The old post holds a public museum and the Fort Meade National Cemetery. Interior not checked.',
  'Where the anthem was first stood for.',
  accn='The Old Fort Meade Museum is on the old post east of Sturgis; museum hours not checked (its own site is drawn by script). The VA hospital and Guard grounds are working sites. The Fort Meade Recreation Area round it is Bureau of Land Management land.',
  links=[W('Fort Meade (South Dakota)'),['BLM: Fort Meade Recreation Area','https://www.blm.gov/visit/fort-meade-recreation-area']],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('fort-pierre-chouteau-site','Fort Pierre Chouteau site','Fort Pierre','sd','fort','marker',44.38917,-100.39111,'site','public','walk','forts','1832–1857',
  'A fur-trading post of the American Fur Company, ordered built in 1832 by Pierre Chouteau Jr. when he came up the Missouri on the first trip of the steamboat Yellowstone; it replaced Fort Tecumseh near the river mouth. In the 1850s 100,000 buffalo robes a year went out through it. Chouteau sold it to the Army in 1854, which found it poor and left in 1857 for Fort Randall. The ground was pasture until the state bought it in 1930; it became a National Historic Landmark in 1991.',
  'No buildings are left. A gravel path leads to a stone marker near the middle of the site (Wikipedia), and markers on the grounds tell of the fort and the digs that found its oldest floor (HMdb).',
  'A hundred thousand buffalo robes a year passed here.',
  accn='Owned by the State of South Dakota since 1930 (Wikipedia); the federal land map does not hold it. A walking trail runs from Fort Chouteau Road, 350 Fort Chouteau Road (HMdb). Hours not checked.',
  links=[W('Fort Pierre Chouteau'),HM('124300','Fort Pierre Chouteau trading post'),HM('124484','Archaeology at Fort Pierre Chouteau')],
  th=[44.38914,-100.38516],thn='Fort Chouteau Road, at the start of the walking trail',brick='no'),
N('ingalls-house-de-smet','Ingalls House','De Smet','sd','landmark','standing',44.38538,-97.55295,'exact','open','walk','','1887–',
  'The town house Charles Ingalls built in De Smet in 1887; the family moved in on Christmas Eve that year and lived in it until 1928. Laura, who had married Almanzo Wilder in 1885, never lived here; the family had earlier lived in the surveyors\' house in town in 1879 and 1880. The Laura Ingalls Wilder Memorial Society bought it in 1967 and opened it the next year.',
  'Furniture made by Charles Ingalls is shown in the house (Wikipedia). Charles, Caroline, Mary, Carrie and Grace Ingalls are buried in the De Smet Cemetery about a mile away. Interior fabric beyond that not checked.',
  'The house the Little Town on the Prairie family built.',
  accn='Laura Ingalls Wilder Memorial Society, 210 3rd Street Southwest. Tour times and prices not checked: the society\'s site would not open.',
  links=[W('Ingalls House (De Smet, South Dakota)'),['Laura Ingalls Wilder Memorial Society','https://discoverlaura.org/visit']],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('tinton-ghost-town','Tinton','Spearfish','sd','town','ruins',44.38054,-104.04854,'area','view','drive','','1876–1950s',
  'A gold camp founded in 1876 at the Wyoming line; tin was found in the same year, gold in Negro Hill in 1879, and tin in the pegmatite rock in 1884, which turned the camp to tin. It was a company town by 1904 under a string of tin companies, and from 1937 the home of the Dakota Tin and Gold Mine, listed on the National Register in 2005. Mining faded and the town was empty by the 1950s.',
  'Many buildings still stand, some fallen in, with no glass in the windows (Wikipedia). The Wyoming side, where the hotel, school and post office stood, was rebuilt for modern mining.',
  'A tin town in gold country.',
  accn='The town is managed by a mining company, Tinton Enterprises (Wikipedia): private. Reached by National Forest Road 222; look from the road. Pin is the Wikipedia coordinate for the town.',
  links=[W('Tinton, South Dakota')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
]
